 
                    Chromosome 10 NPL Analyis            
 
    Results of the Non-Parametric Linkage (NPL) Analysis from SimWalk2 2.83
 
 
 In the table below, at each marker locus, the first line contains the values
 for each of five statistics, named A, B, C, D and E. Before describing these
 statistics the other items in the table are explained.
 
 Below the actual value for each statistic is the empirical mean for that
 statistic when the trait is unlinked, given the same pedigree and marker
 structure as the input data.
 
 Below this mean value is the empirical p-value for the actual value obtained,
 i.e., the probability of obtaining the actual, or more extreme, value if the
 trait were in fact unlinked to the markers. Thus, p-values closer to 0.0
 would indicate higher significance. Currently, the minimum p-value is  0.0001
 [to lower this minimum, increase batch item #48: min = 1 / batch-item-#48].
 A p-value of 0.5 indicates equal evidence for and against linkage. As usual,
 the maximum p-value is 1.0.
 
 Finally, the -log10(p-value) is listed. Here, the larger the value, the more
 significant. These values have a minimum of 0.0, and a maximum of  4.0000
 [to raise this maximum, increase batch item #48: max = log10(batch-item-#48)].
 A value of 0.3010 [from: -log10(0.5)] indicates equal evidence for and against
 linkage.
 
 
 The five statistics measure the degree of clustering among the affecteds
 of the founder-alleles, i.e., the marker alleles descending from the founders.
 Specifically, at each marker:
 
 Statistic A is the number of different founder-alleles contributing
 alleles to the affecteds.
 
 Statistic B is the maximum number of alleles among the affecteds
 descended from any one founder-allele.
 
 Statistic C is the 'entropy' of the marker alleles among the affecteds.
 
 Statistic D is the extent of allele sharing among all affected pairs
 as measured by their IBD kinship coefficient.
 
 Statistic E is the NPL_all statistic as implemented in GeneHunter.
 
 When using statistics A and C clustering is suggested by a small value.
 When using statistics B, D and E clustering is suggested by a large value.
 (The evaluation of the p-value and -log(p) terms are discussed above.)
 Statistic A is most powerful at detecting linkage to a recessive trait.
 Statistic B is most powerful at detecting linkage to a dominant trait.
 Statistics C, D and E are more general statistics indicating whether
 a few founder-alleles are overly represented among the affecteds.
 
 More information on statistics A - D, including the formula used for the
 'entropy' term, can be found in Sobel and Lange (1996) American Jounal of
 Human Genetics 58:1323-1337. More information on statistic E can be found in
 Whittemore and Halpern (1994) Biometrics 50:118-127.
 
 
